WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.202
Hostname validation checks that the certificate matches the service the
client intended to reach.

2
00:00:05.202 --> 00:00:13.749
If the Python application connects to a configured service name, the
certificate should identify that service name according to the applicable
validation rules.

3
00:00:13.749 --> 00:00:18.641
This is different from simply proving that the certificate chains to some
trusted authority.

4
00:00:18.641 --> 00:00:24.091
A trusted authority can issue many certificates, and the client needs the
one for the expected endpoint.

5
00:00:24.091 --> 00:00:27.869
Subject alternative names are central in modern certificate identity.

6
00:00:27.869 --> 00:00:34.620
DNS names, service names, wildcard certificates, and IP address
certificates each have specific operational considerations.

7
00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:40.875
Wildcards can simplify management in some designs, but they broaden the
set of names covered by one key and certificate.

8
00:00:40.875 --> 00:00:50.226
IP address certificates and internal service names require careful
planning so clients validate the identity they actually expect instead of
relying on network location alone.

9
00:00:50.226 --> 00:00:55.057
Proxies, gateways, and load balancers can change where certificate
identity is presented.

10
00:00:55.057 --> 00:00:59.392
A client may connect to a gateway name while the gateway routes to a
backend service.

11
00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:02.675
A service mesh may terminate and re-establish TLS internally.

12
00:01:02.675 --> 00:01:05.276
A database proxy may present its own certificate.

13
00:01:05.276 --> 00:01:09.983
These architectures can be safe, but the expected service identity should
be documented.

14
00:01:09.983 --> 00:01:14.504
The certificate name should match the boundary the client is intentionally
trusting.

15
00:01:14.504 --> 00:01:18.281
Certificate name mismatches should be treated as meaningful failures.

16
00:01:18.281 --> 00:01:25.651
Local development may use test names or local-only certificates, but those
exceptions should not normalize insecure production behavior.

17
00:01:25.651 --> 00:01:33.702
Hostname validation matters even when transport is encrypted because it
tells the client whether the encrypted connection is with the intended
service.

18
00:01:33.702 --> 00:01:39.400
Service identity should be based on expected names and trust anchors, not
only on private network placement.
